$ Milestone 5 (docs/architecture.md §3.5): `do`/`grabe`, backed by real
$ LLVM invoke/landingpad + the Itanium C++ exception ABI (see
$ runtime/eh.cpp and codegen.cpp's genDoGrabe). Exercises: a runtime error
$ thrown directly inside a `do` block, one thrown several frames down
$ inside a called `job`, nested do/grabe (an error re-thrown from inside a
$ `grabe` block reaching the OUTER `grabe`), and a `do` block that
$ completes with no error at all (grabe_body must not run).

job safeDivide(a, b) {
    give a / b
}

$ 1. Error thrown directly in the do block's own statements.
do {
    x = 10 / 0
    show(x)
} grabe (err) {
    show("caught #1:")
    show(err)
}

$ 2. Error thrown from inside a job called from the do block -- proves
$ unwinding correctly crosses an ordinary (non-invoke) call frame.
do {
    y = safeDivide(20, 0)
    show(y)
} grabe (err) {
    show("caught #2:")
    show(err)
}

$ 3. Nested do/grabe: the inner grabe catches its own error, then itself
$ throws (by calling something that fails), which the OUTER grabe catches.
do {
    do {
        z = 1 % 0
        show(z)
    } grabe (innerErr) {
        show("caught #3 (inner):")
        show(innerErr)
        w = safeDivide(1, 0) $ throws again -- should reach the outer grabe
        show(w)
    }
} grabe (outerErr) {
    show("caught #3 (outer):")
    show(outerErr)
}

$ 4. A `do` block with no error at all: grabe_body must NOT run, and
$ execution continues normally afterward.
do {
    show("no error in this do block")
    r = safeDivide(10, 2)
    show(r)
} grabe (err) {
    show("should never print")
}

show("all do/grabe cases completed")
